<h1 class="article-title">Liepāja SEZ Board Decisions of 25 September</h1>
<div class="block_text mt-5 mb-4">
<div class="row">
<div class="col"><div class="col-12 output"><p>At its regular meeting on 25 September this year, the Liepāja SEZ Board adopted decisions on an investment agreement, waiving rights of first refusal, an auction and amendments to earlier decisions. It also approved the updated Liepāja Special Economic Zone Development Plan for 2018–2035 and the Port of Liepāja Development Programme for 2023–2027.</p><p>Updating the Liepāja SEZ Development Plan</p><p>The Board approved updated versions of the Liepāja SEZ Development Plan for 2018–2035 and the Port of Liepāja Development Programme for 2023–2027, adapting them to market changes, the geopolitical situation and new development directions.</p><p>The revision combines the two planning documents into one, assesses previous development results, updates the statistical database and regulatory framework, and adds new priorities including renewable and alternative-energy projects.</p><p>The plan aims to strengthen Liepāja as an industrial, logistics and energy centre by developing the Outer Harbour, offshore wind projects, NATO naval infrastructure and the Liepāja Industrial Park. Green-economy projects, including sustainable aviation fuel, hydrogen and CO₂ infrastructure, are also priorities.</p><p>The Authority emphasises that the updated development plan is essential for sustainable development of the port, industry and business, attracting investment and creating jobs in the region.</p><p>Liepāja SEZ companies invest in development</p><p>The Board plans an investment agreement with Liepāja SEZ company Brimer for EUR 3.36 million to construct a new production and administrative building at 37 Atslēdznieku Street, Liepāja.</p><p>The project is planned for 1 October 2026 to 31 December 2028. The company qualifies as a medium-sized enterprise, with a maximum permissible aid intensity of 50%.</p><p>Brimer Liepaja is part of Norway’s Brimer Group and specialises in the design and manufacture of large fibreglass-composite products, offering services from engineering and design through manufacture, transportation and installation.</p><p>The Board also decided to conclude an investment agreement with Jensen Metal for the project “Purchase of New Production Equipment”. The company plans to invest EUR 133,000 between 30 September 2026 and 31 May 2027. The maximum permissible aid intensity for eligible costs is 40%.</p><p>Paid services at the Port of Liepāja</p><p>The Board approved a price list for paid services, expanding the range available to port companies and other customers. The decision reflects demand from port operators and other market participants and aims to make more efficient use of the Authority’s infrastructure and technical resources.</p><p>The Authority will offer certain paid services outside the port’s core functions, without financing them from port charges. Customers will be able to use hydrographic surveying, hydrographic data post-processing, specialised boat services, oil-contaminated water treatment and equipment rental.</p><p>Prices were set using detailed cost calculations and market analysis to cover costs fully and remain competitive. The new price list will enter into force after publication on the Authority’s website.</p><p>Electricity supply agreement</p><p>The Board supported concluding an electricity supply agreement with Enefit for the period from 1 November 2026 to 30 April 2027.</p><p>Two tenders were received, and Enefit’s offer of EUR 155.73/MWh was judged the most economically advantageous.</p><p>The agreement will provide electricity for the Authority’s needs and for business operations of tenants in the Port of Liepāja and the Liepāja Industrial Park.</p><p>Reliable electricity supply is essential for continuous port and industrial park operations and further development.</p><p>Repeat auction for property at 6 Dūmu Street</p><p>The Board approved the rules for a repeat electronic auction of the property at 6 Dūmu Street after the previous auction failed for lack of bidders. The property is in the Liepāja Industrial Park and consists of a 46,766 m² land plot and a scrap-processing building with a total area of 25,181.7 m². The starting price remains EUR 480,000, based on a valuation by LATIO.</p><p>Disposal of the property is part of the Industrial Park development strategy to attract investment and develop the site. Auction proceeds are intended for further industrial park development and obligations associated with disposal.</p><p>Rights of first refusal</p><p>The Board decided not to exercise its rights of first refusal over a 1.5-hectare land plot at 31A Cukura Street in the SEZ or over properties at 6/9 Vecā ostmala, 1 Fēniksa Street and 23 Jaunā Street, Liepāja.</p><p>After assessing the need for the plots, the Authority concluded that they were not required for its functions. After the transaction, the new owners plan to use the land for their company’s development project.</p></div>
